    "cards": [
        {
            "dot": "cyan",
            "title": "A stream, not a poll",
            "items": [
                "agent.session.watch answers an async iterable, so a handler that streams is the same handler shape as one that returns",
                "The caller picks the envelope with Accept: text/event-stream for a readable one, application/vnd.blong.stream for a binary one",
                "Every frame is sealed with the caller's key, because a transcript is what the connection exists to protect",
                "A keepalive frame keeps an idle proxy from closing a connection the reader still wants, and tells a quiet session from a dead one",
                "A frame carries at most 1 MiB, and the cursor partition:offset is how a reader reconnects without a gap or a repeat"
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "emerald",
            "title": "One topic, one order",
            "items": [
                "Telemetry is produced to one topic keyed by session id, so a session's events share a partition and keep their order",
                "The first batch of a session waits for its delivery report, because the report is the only place a partition is a fact",
                "Kafka is the history; MySQL holds the projection the list and the header read, so a page never replays a topic to draw a row",
                "A deployment with no broker sets the source to memory and the same contract is read from a ring"
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "violet",
            "title": "The runtime owns the loop",
            "items": [
                "A copilot runtime on another host runs its own bash, create, edit and view, against the directory the ticket may touch",
                "One account per host, each with its own runtime token; a kind that cannot be reached is an account nobody claims on",
                "The runtime's session id is kept on the session row, so a later resume continues the same conversation rather than a new one",
                "The account carries the wait (timeoutMs), because the SDK's own 60 s for session.idle is shorter than a task that sleeps"
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "amber",
            "title": "The realm answers what the runtime cannot",
            "items": [
                "askHuman and finish are the realm's tools, offered to the runtime as custom tools that dispatch back into agent.tool.run",
                "The adapter's dispatch answers with the framework's payload-and-meta pair, and the tool call id travels with it so a resume matches the pending call",
                "A question that parks a run writes the prompt, the tool call and the history into a checkpoint before it waits",
                "The answer's arrival publishes on a wake channel, and the orchestrator that listens resumes the run in the same process that parked it"
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "rose",
            "title": "Bounded by the workspace, the clock and the grammar",
            "items": [
                "Every file and process goes through one workspace adapter: a path outside the ticket's directory is refused before it is opened",
                "A deployment can switch commands off, and the switch removes the runtime's shell tools rather than trusting a permission prompt",
                "Telemetry is a plain vocabulary, so the page, the projection and a test read one shape whichever kind wrote it",
                "A tool call is read into what it did - command, exit code, output, diff, answer - so the page draws a card rather than JSON"
            ]
        }
    ]
